PROPOSAL (NOT APPLIED to production) — cap the explicit --config size in config.Load
======================================================================================

Problem
-------
internal/config.Load caps the IMPLICIT $HOME/.dsd.yaml at maxImplicitConfigBytes
(1 MiB) via implicitConfigSafe, but the EXPLICIT --config path is deliberately
uncapped ("a deliberate operator choice"). Because the YAML mapping decode is
O(n^2) in sibling-key count (MC4 finding F1; root cause in go.yaml.in/yaml/v3,
see .scratch/upstream-drafts/F1-viper-yaml-quadratic.md), a large --config YAML
is a CPU-exhaustion amplifier: a flat ~1 MiB YAML (~100k short keys) costs tens
of seconds of CPU; a 64k-key doc measured at 32 s. The file need not be hostile —
a generated or mistaken --config is enough.

Proposal
--------
Apply a size cap to the explicit path too, as defense-in-depth that bounds the
quadratic's blast radius. Keep the symlink/permission gate implicit-only (an
explicit path IS an operator choice), but a size bound is not a trust decision —
it protects the operator's own process from a pathological decode. Follow Load's
existing house style on a bad config: fall back to defaults AND return the error
so the caller discloses it (never silently fail a health run over config).

The real fix is upstream (O(1) duplicate-key handling in the YAML decoder); this
cap is the bound dsd can ship today without waiting on that.

